tellraw @a {"text": "Technology: Shaduf", "color": "gold", "bold": true}
tellraw @a {"text": "Three shadufs lifting water in series from a canal to a terrace garden.", "color": "white"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Egypt and Mesopotamia, attested c. 2000 BCE Mesopotamia; Egypt by the New Kingdom (c. 1500 BCE)", "color": "yellow"}
tellraw @a {"text": "A counterweighted lever on a post with a bucket on one end: the oldest machine for lifting", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "irrigation water. It is still in use today.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "A pole pivots on a crossbar; a bucket hangs from the long arm, a clay or stone", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "counterweight sits on the short arm. The worker pulls the bucket down into the river, and", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "the counterweight does most of the lifting as it swings up. One person can raise roughly 2", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "to 3 cubic metres an hour by 1 to 2 metres; several shadufs in series lift higher.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "See it in context: /function ac:egypt/nile", "color": "aqua"}
